==English==

===Etymology===
Of {{unc|en|nocap=1}} origin. Earliest known comes from San Francisco (in 1855) at the time of the {{w|California Gold Rush}}. These possibilities have been suggested:
* {{der|en|fr|manigance|ces manigances|t=these fraudulent schemes}}.
* {{der|en|es|chanada}}, shortening of {{m|es|charranada||trick, deceit}}.
* {{der|en|ga|sionnachuighim|t=I play the fox}}.<ref name="dictionary.com">[https://www.dictionary.com/browse/shenanigan Word Origin and History for shenanigan] at Dictionary.com</ref>
* {{der|en|gmw-rfr|schinägeln|t=to work hard}}, from the peddler's argot term {{m|gmw-rfr||Schenigelei|t=work}}.<ref name="dictionary.com"/>
* {{w|East Anglian English|East Anglian}} dialect {{m|en|nannicking|t=playing the fool}}.
* 18th century {{der|en|de|Scheinheilige|pos=noun plural|t=sham holy men / sham holy actions}}, {{m|de|scheinheilig|t=hypocritical}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ʃəˈnæn.ɪˌɡən/}},
* {{audio|en|shenanigan_us.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable}} A deceitful confidence trick, or mischief causing discomfort or annoyance.
#: {{ux|en|I spotted his next '''shenanigan'''&nbsp;– saw it coming&nbsp;– and so avoided being fooled.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|dated|rare}} {{singular of|en|shenanigans}}.
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Mr. Soap’s Yachting Excursion|magazine=Yankee-notions|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Published by T. W. Strong, 98 [[w:Nassau Street (Manhattan)|Nassau-st.]]|month=September|year=1855|volume=IV|issue=9|page=280|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=7JFRAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA280|oclc=32423418|passage=One of Professor [John] Moon's most astonishing "experiments," consisted of holding a watch suspended from a short chain at arm's length, and allowing anyone in the pit to pull pistol and "blaze away" at the word "fire," whereupon the watch would most unconscionably disappear. An individual who had attended several evenings and witnessed the "experiment," suspected, in the classic language of the times, that there was something of "'''shenanigan'''" in it.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Cha[rle]s H. Sparks|chapter=Political History|title=History of Winneshiek County, with Biographical Sketches of Its Eminent Men|location=Decorah, Iowa|publisher=Jas. Alex. Leonard|year=[1877]|page=31|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=MI4UAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA31|oclc=995345986|passage=There are stories still told how money was used and promised, but from the best knowledge I can acquire, I think this is not true. If sharp practice was played, and "'''shenanigan'''" was used, we, to-day, looking back upon those times, cannot say that evil has come of it.}}

